#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ebe5738-7682-55ad-a21b-235063f08b88" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Period of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "9a8dfc78-929c-525b-9403-7c1d11d8713f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1124" :question/problem "What is the period of the function shown below?\n\n![](images/07/0780a6b1bd6f6d738c9e80e13757ff24d688728aaf8ac306284761b0e1124764.png)" :question/worked-solution "Note that if we shift the graph $3$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $3$ (or a multiple of $3$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57b95b5b-8834-5d1a-8380-fd8889e05cb4" :knowledge-point/title "Drawing the Graph of a Periodic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1529ba15-ecf2-5c3f-871d-ab249ef9af34" :question/math-academy-id "e-1125" :question/problem "Part of the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ with period $2$ is shown below. What is the graph of $y=f(x)$ on the interval $-4\\leq x \\leq 4$?\n\n![](images/59/59abf18dc978f48da4bec95bc702141b2459a327f194fda46d64f12c5bc8ffb4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The graph above shows the function in the interval\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nSince this interval has length $2$, and the period of the function is also $2$, this interval represents a single period of the function.\n\nTherefore, the function will consist of many copies of the shape shown above, shifted horizontally by multiples of $2$.\n\n![](images/41/41a583651bf690810335776b9e7b5620e6b6830e1b59ee9d646c4ab429e17de8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "282b873c-1ba0-5205-b75f-c58721ffaaff" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Values of a Periodic Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "6f991ce1-e19a-5324-830a-a17f7ac22848" :question/math-academy-id "e-1126" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Given that $f$ is periodic with period $4$, what is the value of $f(84)$?\n\n![](images/b7/b763649968dc1a117007eba0b7cb0862473aadf702e8c91fc30ac83149445865.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the period is $4$, if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(84) &= f(84 - 4) \\\\\n&= f(84 - 2 \\cdot 4) \\\\\n&= ⋯ \\\\\n&= f(84 - 20 \\cdot 4) \\\\\n&= f(4) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
